{"title":"Linear algebra","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Linear algebra</b> is the study of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_space\" title=\"Vector space\">vector spaces</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Linear_transformation&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Linear transformation (page does not exist)\">linear transformations</a> between them.  This viewpoint is motivated by the study of systems of linear equations.  Because systems of linear equations are very well-understood, in some sense, many other areas of mathematics and science attempt to reformulate problems into a linear algebra problems where they may be easier to solve.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Reformulation_of_systems_of_linear_equations_as_linear_transformations\">Reformulation of systems of linear equations as linear transformations</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Linear_algebra&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Reformulation of systems of linear equations as linear transformations\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose we have the system of linear equations\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/3/0d32663f13dc86685d9a7a2cb963c8c7ec2f2820.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} x + 2y &amp;= 3, \\\\ 2x - 3y &amp;= -2. \\end{align*}\" width=\"110\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>As one learns in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Algebra\" title=\"Algebra\">algebra</a>, these equations can be solved for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> through various means.  An important property that these methods depend on is that the solution(s) do not change if we add equations or subtract constant multiples of equations from one another.  For instance, in the above example, we can subtract the twice the first equation from the second to obtain\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/8/c58a2b7509bc4eb6389860ac6ae0273bcb8d923f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} x + 2y &amp;= 3, \\\\    -7y &amp;= -8. \\end{align*}\" width=\"101\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>Another important property is that we can scale both sides of an equation by a nonzero constant without changing the solutions of the overall system.  More plainly stated, in the second equation, we would \"scale\" both sides of the equation by multiplying by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/8/8d8397f2567d827cc672485954007567dc4a263e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1/7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"43\" height=\"20\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/5/58544bcba080755f100135172874778a11c79209.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 8/7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"20\" />.  From there, solving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> amounts to substituting for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> to obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/a/74a972247528133d5ed8e91af13eb96107ea432b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 5/7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Linear algebra takes these ideas and uses the language of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Functions\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Functions\">functions</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector\" title=\"Vector\">vectors</a> to reformulate the above in a more rigorous fashion.  In the above example, we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> be the function with domain the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cartesian_plane\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Cartesian plane\">Cartesian plane</a> (that is, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/a/00a62d72646c467ae02878bc6dd7138e087a13c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^2$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />) and outputs in the Cartesian plane (that is, the [Codomain|codomain] is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/a/00a62d72646c467ae02878bc6dd7138e087a13c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^2$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />) defined by \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/c/4dc5c3c57b16fdb781e66e32e7548eeab0ebf260.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\right) = \\begin{bmatrix}x + 2y \\\\ 2x - 3y\\end{bmatrix}.\\]\" width=\"178\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Thus, our original system of linear equations can be rewritten as \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/a/9bae6c6f20683b45338c8dd54e09e8bb4472babf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\right) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\]\" width=\"141\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>In the language of functions, what we did in the more plainly stated example of system of linear equations is we found that the vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/a/eaaa685d0fe5c45904c05078893311c8504b5f7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"44\" height=\"45\" /> is in the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Range\" title=\"Range\">range</a> of the function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> (because plugging in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/a/74a972247528133d5ed8e91af13eb96107ea432b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 5/7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/5/58544bcba080755f100135172874778a11c79209.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 8/7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"20\" /> makes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> spit out this particular vector).  In addition, to this, we can replicate the procedure we did to solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> via this framework.  To do so, we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the function from the Cartesian plane to the Cartesian plane given by the rule\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/d/88d2fc1b321aba5f88ee15d7935cd99ee2b1b19f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\right) = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y - 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\]\" width=\"172\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Stated in plain English, this function corresponds to subtracting twice first equation from the second equation.  And it does this by  taking our equation\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/8/188d6e65a75b2246541a0d87be344386861e39ed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\right) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\]\" width=\"131\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>and applying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to both sides to get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/1/03143ab1d7331118c986111e0effcc81d8fcc928.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[T\\left(f\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\right)\\right) = T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right).\\]\" width=\"226\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>If the reader works out both sides of the equation, we will get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/c/f7c06607272556326e79d4abfc91c21a1fac623c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{bmatrix}x + 2y \\\\ -7y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\]\" width=\"138\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>There are corresponding functions that transform the system of linear equations further to solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> explicitly.  The functions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> are both examples of <b>linear transformations</b>.  The reader may notice that these operations are very similar to <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Matrix_row_operations&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Matrix row operations (page does not exist)\">matrix row operations</a> from algebra (more precisely, both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> can be represented by matrices in the sense that their formulas are given by matrix-vector multiplication).\n</p><p>The theory of linear algebra is based upon the properties that these linear transformations have (both geometrically and algebraically).  These theories allow us to answer questions like:\n</p>\n<ul><li>When do systems of linear equations have infinitely many solutions?  Just one?  None?</li>\n<li>What properties do solutions of linear equations have?</li>\n<li>What interactions are there between the geometry and algebra of systems of linear equations?</li>\n<li>What changes in the above questions if we allow for solutions to live in different number systems (like the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complex_numbers\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Complex numbers\">complex numbers</a> or <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Finite_field\" title=\"Finite field\">finite fields</a>).</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Linear_algebra&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Matrix\" title=\"Matrix\">Matrix</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Determinant\" title=\"Determinant\">Determinant</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_space\" title=\"Vector space\">Vector space</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inner_product\" title=\"Inner product\">Inner product</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Linear_algebra&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017192759\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.021 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.033 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 187/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 283/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6190/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.397      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.397      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4651:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017192759 and revision id 286810. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}